<article xml:id="week6" label="0006"> <!-- Change label to a random string of numbers for each worksheet. The label shows up in the url of the website and keeps students from searching for other worksheets -->
  <title>MATH 140 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!--START TYPING CONTENT FOR SESSION BELOW HERE-->
  <worksheet>
    <title> Session #6 (Sections 4.2, 4.3, &amp; 4.4, Exam II Review)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you.
      The Math 140 textbook is free, and can be viewed at <url href="https://oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/7d9f5efe-b63b-4c57-8db3-91790deed00d" visual="oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/7d9f5efe-b63b-4c57-8db3-91790deed00d">Texas A&amp;M OAKTrust Digital Repository</url>.
    </p>
    <paragraphs> <!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
      <exercise><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Recall that, for a uniform sample space <m>S</m>, the probability of an event, <m>A</m> (denoted <m>P(A)</m>) is calculated as
            <md>
              P(A)=\dfrac{\text{total number of outcomes in }A}{\text{total number of possible outcomes}}.
            </md>
            A bag contains the same size marbles in three different colors: red, blue, and white. The bag has <m>14</m> identical red marbles, <m>12</m> identical blue marbles, and <m>20</m> identical white marbles (all of the same size).
            One marble is drawn from the bag and the color of the marble is noted. Let <m>B</m> be the event that the marble drawn is blue, <m>R</m> be the event that the marble drawn is red, and <m>W</m> the event that the marble drawn is white.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              What is the total number of possible outcomes for this experiment?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>46</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>P(B)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(B)=\dfrac{12}{46}=\dfrac{6}{23}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the probability the marble is red or white.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(R\cup W) = \dfrac{34}{46}=\dfrac{17}{23}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>P(W^c)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(W^c)=\dfrac{26}{46}=\dfrac{13}{23}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise><!-- #2 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Answer True or False to each of the following questions.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The probability of any event is always between <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>, inclusive.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              True
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If two events, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, are mutually exclusive, then <m>P(A \cap B)=\emptyset</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              False
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For any two events, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, <m>P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              False
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The complement rule states that <m>P(A^c)=1-P(A)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              True
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The sum of probabilities of all possible outcomes in a sample space is <m>1</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              True
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise><!-- #3 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let the sample space for an experiment be <m>S=\{s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4,s_5\}</m>. Given the probability distribution below, what is <m>P(s_3)</m>?
            <table>
                <title>Sample space and probabilities for Section 1 Exercise 3</title>
                <tabular halign="center">
                    <row bottom="major" header="no">
                        <cell>Outcomes</cell>
                        <cell><m>s_1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_3</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_5</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="major">
                        <cell>Probability</cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{3}{10}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{5}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>?</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{20}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{10}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(s_3)=\dfrac{7}{20}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A real estate agent has kept records of the number of houses with one or two bedrooms, three bedrooms, four bedrooms, and five or more bedrooms that they sold during the years <m>2016</m> to <m>2020</m>. The data is listed in the following table.
            <table>
                <title>Number of bedrooms per house versus year</title>
                <tabular halign="center">
                    <row bottom="major" header="yes">
                        <cell>Year</cell>
                        <cell>One or Two</cell>
                        <cell>Three</cell>
                        <cell>Four</cell>
                        <cell>Five or more</cell>
                        <cell>Total</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>2016</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>7</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>15</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>22</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>47</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>2017</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>6</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>19</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>25</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>54</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>2018</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>17</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>24</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>48</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>2019</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>9</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>16</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>22</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>51</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>2020</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>18</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>30</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>6</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>59</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="major">
                        <cell>Total</cell>
                        <cell><m>32</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>85</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>123</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>19</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>259</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            If a home sold by the agent is selected at random, what is the probability...
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              ... the home had exactly four bedrooms?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{123}{259}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              ... the home was sold after <m>2017</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{158}{259}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              ... the home was not sold in <m>2020</m> and had five or more bedrooms?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{13}{259}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              ... the home was not sold in <m>2016</m> or was not sold in <m>2020</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{259}{259}=1</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two events such that <m>P(A)=0.4</m>, <m>P(B)=0.3</m>, and <m>P(A \cup B)=0.5</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>P(A \cap B)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(A \cap B) = 0.2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>P(A^c \cup B)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(A^c \cup B) = 0.8</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>P((A\cup B)^c)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P((A\cup B)^c)=0.5</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let the sample space for an experiment be <m>S=\{s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4,s_5,s_6\}</m>, with events <m>A=\{s_2,s_4,s_5\}</m> and <m>B=\{s_1,s_3,s_4\}</m>, and the probability distribution below.
            <table>
                <title>Sample space and probabilities for Section 2 Exercise 3</title>
                <tabular halign="center">
                    <row bottom="major" header="no">
                        <cell>Outcomes</cell>
                        <cell><m>s_1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_3</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>s_6</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="major">
                        <cell>Probability</cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{3}{20}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{10}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{5}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>?</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{3}{20}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{20}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>P(A^c)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(A^c)=\dfrac{8}{20}=\dfrac{2}{5}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>P(A\cup B)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(A\cup B)=\dfrac{19}{20}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>P(A^c\cap B)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>P(A^c\cap B)=\dfrac{7}{20}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              A cereal company released <m>2000</m> special boxes at a price of <m>\$2</m> each. Within these boxes, the company randomly distributes one <m>\$1000</m> coupon, two <m>\$200</m> coupons, and three <m>\$100</m> coupons. Compute your expected net winnings from buying a single special box of this cereal. Is this game fair?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>E(X)=-\$1.15</m>, this game is not fair.
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              A new piano is insured with a full-replacement policy worth <m>\$12000</m> if the piano is damaged beyond repair. If the piano is damaged, but able to be repaired, the policy will pay half of the full-replacement value. There is a <m>1.5\%</m> chance the piano will need to be replaced during the year and a <m>2.5\%</m> chance it will be damaged but can be repaired. If the premium for this policy is <m>\$1000</m>, find the insurance company's expected profit.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>E(X)=\$670</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Set up, but do not solve, the following linear programming problem.

              A small woodworking business produces three types of furniture items: tables, chairs, and desks. Each product 
              requires a certain amount of labor, wood, and workspace. Tables require <m>4</m> hours of labor, <m>3</m> units of wood, and 
              occupy <m>5</m> square feet of workspace while being assembled. Chairs require <m>2</m> hours of labor, <m>1</m> unit of wood, and <m>2</m>
              square feet of workspace while being assembled. Desks require <m>5</m> hours of labor, <m>4</m> units of wood, and <m>6</m> square 
              feet of workspace while being assembled. The business has <m>200</m> labor hours, <m>150</m> units of wood, and <m>234</m> square 
              feet of usable workspace. The business makes <m>\$30</m> in profit for each table, <m>\$12</m> for each chair, and <m>\$40</m> for each 
              desk it produces. How many of each type of furniture should the company produce to obtain maximum profit?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>x \amp =\text{\# of tables produced by the business}</mrow>
                <mrow>y \amp =\text{\# of chairs produced by the business}</mrow>
                <mrow>z \amp =\text{\# of desks produced by the business}</mrow>
                <mrow>P \amp =\text{Profit (in dollars) for the produced furniture}</mrow>
              </md>
              <md>
                <mrow>\text{Maximize } \amp P=30x+12y+40z</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{subject to } \amp 4x+2y+5z\leq 200</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 3x+y+4z\leq 150</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 5x+2y+6z\leq 234</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x\geq 0, y\geq 0, z\geq 0</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Consider the linear programming problem below.
              <md>
                <mrow>\text{Maximize } \amp P=6x+5y</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{subject to } \amp 2x+y\leq 40</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x+3y\leq 45</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 0\leq x \leq 18</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 0\leq y \leq 20</mrow>
              </md>
              Graph the solution set <m>S</m>. Label and identify all corner points.
            <image source="h6/images/h6fig1" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Coordinate axes. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  Coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-50</m> to <m>50</m>.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
            <image source="h6/images/h6fig2" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of the linear programming problem above with solution set and corner points marked. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of the linear programming problem above, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-50</m> to <m>50</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All corner points of the solution set have been marked with solid dots and their coordinates below.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
            Corner points: <m>\{(0,0),(0,15),(15,10),(18,4),(18,0)\}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="8"><!-- #8 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              The feasible region, <m>S</m>, shaded in the graph below 
            <image source="h6/images/h6fig3" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of the linear programming problem below with solution set and corner points marked. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of the linear programming problem above, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-3</m> to <m>13</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-1</m> to <m>10</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All corner points of the solution set have been marked with solid dots. Each equation is placed next to its corresponding graph.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
              corresponds to the following system of inequalities.
            <md>\begin{cases}9x+2y\geq 30\\ y\leq 3x\\ 2x+10y\geq 21\\ 18x-9y\leq 90\end{cases}</md>
              Use the method of corners to find the maximum and minimum, if they exist, for the objective function <m>P=2x+3y</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              No maximum, minimum at <m>\left(3,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="9"><!-- #9 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              The following linear programming problem can be written as a standard maximization problem. Create the initial simplex tableau for the problem and find the first pivot element.
              <md>
                <mrow>\text{Maximize } \amp P = 4x-5y+z</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{subject to } \amp 4x+7z\leq 50</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x+3y+2z\leq 30</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp 5y\leq 120+3z</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp x\geq 0, y\geq 0, z\geq 0</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y \amp z \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  4 \amp 0 \amp 7 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 50 \\
                  1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 30 \\
                  0 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 120 \\
                  \hline
                  -4 \amp 5 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
                \end{array}
              </me>
              The first pivot element is the value <m>4</m> in row <m>1</m>, column <m>1</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 3  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
      
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose you have an unbounded feasible region, <m>R</m>, in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. Region <m>R</m> has corner points at <m>(7,0)</m>, <m>(4,6)</m>, <m>(9,1)</m>, and <m>(5,10)</m>. Given the objective function <m>P=3x+4y</m>, find the maximum value for <m>P</m> on this feasible region, <m>R</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a maximum of <m>55</m> when <m>x=5</m> and <m>y=10</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a maximum of <m>62</m> when <m>x=6</m> and <m>y=11</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a maximum of <m>73</m> when <m>x=4</m> and <m>y=6</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a maximum at all points <m>(x,y)</m> in <m>R</m> because it is unbounded.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has no maximum because <m>R</m> is unbounded.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (E)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose you have an unbounded feasible region, <m>R</m>, in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. Region <m>R</m> has corner points at <m>(7,0)</m>, <m>(4,6)</m>, <m>(9,1)</m>, and <m>(5,10)</m>. Given the objective function <m>P=3x+4y</m>, find the minimum value for <m>P</m> on this feasible region, <m>R</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a minimum of <m>31</m> when <m>x=9</m> and <m>y=1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a minimum of <m>0</m> when <m>x=0</m> and <m>y=0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a minimum of <m>21</m> when <m>x=7</m> and <m>y=0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has a minimum at all points <m>(x,y)</m> in <m>R</m> because it is unbounded.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P</m> has no minimum because <m>R</m> is unbounded.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            One card is drawn from a well-shuffled, standard deck of <m>52</m> cards. If <m>E</m> is the event "a queen is drawn" and <m>F</m> is the event "a red card is drawn", which is true about the event <m>E \cap F</m>?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>E</m> and <m>F</m> are mutually exclusive, so <m>P(E\cap F)=0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P(E \cap F)=\dfrac{28}{52}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P(E \cap F)=\dfrac{30}{52}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>P(E \cap F)=\dfrac{2}{52}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  None of the above are true.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            A small farm plants two crops: kale and tomatoes. The farm has up to <m>20</m> acres of land, <m>48000</m> gallons of
            water, and <m>240</m> hours of labor it can divide between these two crops. Each crop of kale requires <m>2</m> acres of
            land, <m>2000</m> gallons of water, <m>12</m> hours of labor, and makes <m>\$4,000</m> in profit. Each crop of tomatoes requires <m>1</m>
            acre of land, <m>3000</m> gallons of water, <m>16</m> hours of labor, and makes <m>\$6,000</m> in profit. The farmers have
            calculated they achieve a maximum profit of <m>\$88,000</m> when they plant <m>4</m> crops of kale and <m>12</m> crops of
            tomatoes. Given this solution, how much of each resource (land, water, and labor) is left over?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>4</m> acres of land, <m>4000</m> gallons of water, and <m>12</m> hours of labor
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>4</m> acres of land, <m>2000</m> gallons of water, and <m>0</m> hours of labor
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>4</m> acres of land, <m>4000</m> gallons of water, and <m>0</m> hours of labor
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m> acres of land, <m>4000</m> gallons of water, and <m>0</m> hours of labor
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m> acres of land, <m>2000</m> gallons of water, and <m>0</m> hours of labor
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Which system of linear inequalities is represented by the graph below?
            <image source="h6/images/h6fig4" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Graph of a linear programming problem with solution set and corner points marked. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription><!--This is what a screenreader reads. Do not include math mode here.-->
              <description><!--this is not automatically read by a screenreader, but user can navigate there. Math mode is acceptable here and paragraph environment must be included-->
                <p>
                  A graph of a linear programming problem, on coordinate axes with <m>x</m>-values from <m>-2</m> to <m>20</m> and <m>y</m>-values from <m>-4</m> to <m>14</m>.
                  The solution set is marked with an <m>S</m>. All corner points of the solution set have been marked with solid dots at <m>(6,4)</m>, <m>(8,8)</m>, and <m>(12,7)</m>.
                  The line passing through <m>(6,4)</m> and <m>(8,8)</m> is solid. The line passing through <m>(8,8)</m> and <m>(12,7)</m> is dashed. The line passing through <m>(6,4)</m> and <m>(12,7)</m> is dashed.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{cases}-x-4y\leq -40\\ x-2y\leq -2\\ -2x+y\lt -8 \end{cases}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{cases}-x-4y\gt -40\\ x-2y\lt -2\\ -2x+y\leq -8 \end{cases}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{cases}-x-4y\geq -40\\ x-2y\leq -2\\ -2x+y\leq -8 \end{cases}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{cases}-x-4y\lt -40\\ x-2y\gt -2\\ -2x+y\leq -8 \end{cases}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  None of these.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine whether the simplex tableau below is in its final form. If it is, find the optimal solution. If it is not, state the next pivot entry.
              <me>
                \begin{array}{rrrrrr|r}
                  x \amp y  \amp s_1 \amp s_2 \amp s_3 \amp P \amp c \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 1 \amp \dfrac{1}{8} \amp 0 \amp -\dfrac{1}{8} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{1}{4} \\
                  0 \amp 0 \amp -\dfrac{1}{4} \amp 1 \amp \dfrac{7}{4} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{35}{2} \\
                  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                  \hline
                  0 \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{3}{8} \amp 0 \amp \dfrac{13}{8} \amp 1 \amp \dfrac{35}{4}
                \end{array}
              </me>
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  The tableau is not in final form because of the negative in column <m>\#3</m>. The next pivot element is in row <m>\#1</m>, column <m>\#3</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The tableau is not in final form because of the negative in column <m>\#5</m>. The next pivot element is in row <m>\#2</m>, column <m>\#5</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The tableau is in final form. The optimal solution is <m>x=\dfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>y=\dfrac{35}{2}</m>, <m>s_1=2</m>, <m>s_2=0</m>, <m>s_3=0</m>, and <m>P=\dfrac{35}{4}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The tableau is in final form. The optimal solution is <m>x=2</m>, <m>y=\dfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>s_1=0</m>, <m>s_2=\dfrac{35}{2}</m>, <m>s_3=0</m>, and <m>P=\dfrac{35}{4}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The tableau is in final form. The optimal solution is <m>x=2</m>, <m>y=\dfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>s_1=\dfrac{1}{4}</m>, <m>s_2=0</m>, <m>s_3=0</m>, and <m>P=\dfrac{35}{4}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
  
  </worksheet>

